        -: 1359:
        -: 1360:// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -: 1361:// This class is a "logical time span" and is useful for implementing program
        -: 1362:// logic for such things as "add one month to the date" which, in general,
        -: 1363:// doesn't mean to add 60*60*24*31 seconds to it, but to take the same date
        -: 1364:// the next month (to understand that this is indeed different consider adding
        -: 1365:// one month to Feb, 15 - we want to get Mar, 15, of course).
        -: 1366://
        -: 1367:// When adding a month to the date, all lesser components (days, hours, ...)
        -: 1368:// won't be changed unless the resulting date would be invalid: for example,
        -: 1369:// Jan 31 + 1 month will be Feb 28, not (non existing) Feb 31.
        -: 1370://
        -: 1371:// Because of this feature, adding and subtracting back again the same
        -: 1372:// wxDateSpan will *not*, in general give back the original date: Feb 28 - 1
        -: 1373:// month will be Jan 28, not Jan 31!
        -: 1374://
        -: 1375:// wxDateSpan can be either positive or negative. They may be
        -: 1376:// multiplied by scalars which multiply all deltas by the scalar: i.e. 2*(1
        -: 1377:// month and 1 day) is 2 months and 2 days. They can be added together and
        -: 1378:// with wxDateTime or wxTimeSpan, but the type of result is different for each
        -: 1379:// case.
        -: 1380://
        -: 1381:// Beware about weeks: if you specify both weeks and days, the total number of
        -: 1382:// days added will be 7*weeks + days! See also GetTotalDays() function.
        -: 1383://
        -: 1384:// Equality operators are defined for wxDateSpans. Two datespans are equal if
        -: 1385:// they both give the same target date when added to *every* source date.
        -: 1386:// Thus wxDateSpan::Months(1) is not equal to wxDateSpan::Days(30), because
        -: 1387:// they not give the same date when added to 1 Feb. But wxDateSpan::Days(14) is
        -: 1388:// equal to wxDateSpan::Weeks(2)
        -: 1389://
        -: 1390:// Finally, notice that for adding hours, minutes &c you don't need this
        -: 1391:// class: wxTimeSpan will do the job because there are no subtleties
        -: 1392:// associated with those.
        -: 1393:// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -: 1394:
        -: 1395:class WXDLLIMPEXP_BASE wxDateSpan
        -: 1396:{
        -: 1397:public:
        -: 1398:    // constructors
        -: 1399:    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
        -: 1400:
        -: 1401:        // this many years/months/weeks/days
        -: 1402:    wxDateSpan(int years = 0, int months = 0, int weeks = 0, int days = 0)
        -: 1403:    {
        -: 1404:        m_years = years;
        -: 1405:        m_months = months;
        -: 1406:        m_weeks = weeks;
        -: 1407:        m_days = days;
        -: 1408:    }
        -: 1409:
        -: 1410:        // get an object for the given number of days
